terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply
terraform refresh
terraform destroy

# Option B: Re-apply without changes (updates state and prints new root outputs)
terraform apply -refresh-only

terraform output	
Displays all defined root output values without running a full plan.

terraform state list	
Lists all resources currently tracked inside your terraform.tfstate file.

terraform validate	
Checks your syntax and attribute names in .tf files for errors without calling AWS APIs.

terraform fmt	
Automatically formats your .tf code to follow standard HashiCorp spacing and style rules.

terraform state list -state=terraform.tfstate.backup

terraform init -upgrade

terraform providers

Visualize the Dependency Graph (Optional)
You can inspect the full graph that Terraform generates internally by running:
terraform graph

(This prints a DOT graph showing all directed edges between your resources, including explicit depends_on linkages!)

🛠️ Handy Command: refresh-only
If you ever want to update your local .tfstate file to reflect what's currently in AWS without changing any actual infrastructure, run:

terraform plan -refresh-only
# then if you agree with state update:
terraform apply -refresh-only

If you wish to attempt automatic migration of the state, use 
"terraform init -migrate-state".
│ If you wish to store the current configuration with no changes to the state, use 
"terraform init -reconfigure".